INT. FOURTH-FLOOR LAUNDRY ROOM - APARTMENT BUILDING - NIGHT

Two washers, one broken. A folding table with a wobbly leg. Fluorescent hum.

ADAEZE OBI-LINDGREN (30s, scrubs, tired) folds towels. Her neighbor GRETCHEN VOSKUIJLEN (60s, curlers, quilted robe) watches the dryer spin.

                    GRETCHEN
          You work too much, dear.

                    ADAEZE
          Somebody has to.

                    GRETCHEN
          Mm.

She nods at the dryer. Adaeze keeps folding. A towel. Another towel. A baby's sleeper, tiny.

                    GRETCHEN (CONT'D)
          Is that new?

                    ADAEZE
          Hand-me-down.

                    GRETCHEN
          From your sister?

                    ADAEZE
          From a friend.

The dryer buzzes. Gretchen opens the door and fishes out warm sheets. Adaeze takes one end. They fold together, corner to corner, without a word.

The door opens. A tall boy of thirteen, TEO (13, hoodie, earbuds), pads in with a hamper.

                    TEO
          Mom, you said eleven.

                    ADAEZE
          I know. One more load.

                    TEO
          Hi, Mrs. Voskuijlen.

                    GRETCHEN
          Hello, Teo.

He sets the hamper down and sits cross-legged on the floor. Adaeze hands him the baby's sleeper.

                    ADAEZE
          Fold that for me.

He folds it. Neat. Careful. She watches. Gretchen smiles at the dryer.
